%I A289554 #32 Aug 04 2026 01:18:32
%S A289554 24,32,36,40,48,54,56,60,64,72,80,84,88,90,96,100,104,108,112,120,126,
%T A289554 128,132,135,136,140,144,150,152,156,160,162,168,176,180,184,189,192,
%U A289554 196,198,200,204,208,210,216,220,224,225,228,232,234,240,243,248,250
%N A289554 Numbers that are the product of two distinct composite numbers (A002808).
%C A289554 Numbers that are not the 4th power of a prime but have at least 4 prime factors (counted with multiplicity). - _Robert Israel_, Aug 02 2026
%C A289554 48 is the smallest term that is the product of two distinct composite numbers two different ways: 48 = 4 * 12 = 6 * 8. The smallest odd term is 135, and it is also the smallest term to be immediately followed by another term of this sequence, 136. - _Alonso del Arte_, Aug 02 2026
%H A289554 Robert Israel, <a href="/A289554/b289554.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%e A289554 The number 24 is in the sequence because 24 = 4 * 6.
%e A289554 The numbers 25 and 26 are not in the sequence because they are semiprimes. Although 27 is divisible by 9 and 28 is divisible by 4 and 14, neither 27 nor 28 can be expressed as a product of two composite numbers. 30 has the following nontrivial composite divisors: 6, 10 and 15, but in each case the corresponding divisor is a prime number. And 31 is obviously not in the sequence on account of being a prime number.
%p A289554 filter:= proc(n) local F;
%p A289554   F:= ifactors(n)[2][..,2];
%p A289554   convert(F,`+`) >= 4 and F <> [4]
%p A289554 end proc:
%p A289554 select(filter, [$1..300]); # _Robert Israel_, Aug 02 2026
%o A289554 (Magma) [n: n in [1..1000] | (#PrimeDivisors(n) eq 1 and NumberOfDivisors(n) ge 6) or (#PrimeDivisors(n) eq 2 and NumberOfDivisors(n) ge 8) or (#PrimeDivisors(n) ge 3 and NumberOfDivisors(n) ge 12) or (#PrimeDivisors(n) ge 4)];
%o A289554 (Python)
%o A289554 from sympy import primepi, integer_nthroot
%o A289554 from oeis_sequences.OEISsequences import almostprimepi
%o A289554 def A289554(n):
%o A289554     def f(x): return int(n+1+primepi(integer_nthroot(x,4)[0])+almostprimepi(x,3)+almostprimepi(x,2)+primepi(x))
%o A289554     m, k = n, f(n)
%o A289554     while m != k: m, k = k, f(k)
%o A289554     return m # _Chai Wah Wu_, Aug 03 2026
%Y A289554 Complement of A289555.
%Y A289554 Cf. A006881 (with two distinct primes), A002808 (with two distinct nonprimes), A167171 (with two distinct noncomposites).
%K A289554 nonn
